        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Do treadmills work without a subscription?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Almost all of them run, but what the console does varies enormously. Sole and the commercial brands have no subscription at all. Several ProForm and NordicTrack models keep full manual speed and incline control plus some onboard programs. A few are reduced to a bare manual mode with no programs and no history, which makes the screen you paid for largely useless.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does a treadmill subscription cost over five years?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              At around $39 a month it is roughly $470 a year and about $2,350 over five years — for most machines in this category, more than the treadmill itself cost. A $1,300 subscription-free machine costs $1,300 over five years; an $1,800 machine with that fee costs roughly $4,150. Prices also rise, and your hardware does not get cheaper to keep using when they do.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I use my own apps on a treadmill instead of the subscription?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, and it works better than most people expect. Prop a tablet in front of the machine and run any content you like. You lose automatic incline and speed adjustment — the trainer says raise the incline and you press the button — and you gain a far wider choice of content. If the machine broadcasts metrics over Bluetooth, your history flows into a third-party app too.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which treadmill brands have no subscription at all?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sole is the best-known consumer brand that has never required one, and the commercial manufacturers — Life Fitness, Matrix and similar — do not either, because gyms will not buy equipment that stops working when a contract lapses. Those two groups are where to look if complete independence is the priority.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does a subscription affect treadmill resale value?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, noticeably. Subscription-free machines sell faster second-hand and hold price better, because the buyer is purchasing a working treadmill rather than taking on a monthly commitment. Connected machines can also require the original account to be released before a new owner can register, and that process ranges from simple to genuinely awkward — check before buying, not when selling.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill subscription ever worth paying for?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Genuinely, yes, in two cases. If you will not train without external structure, guided content is the difference between a machine used regularly and a machine used twice, and at that point the comparison is subscription versus not training rather than subscription versus nothing. And automatic incline control during structured hill sessions is the one feature a tablet cannot replicate.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
